+# RSpec performance notes
+
+## Model spec baseline
+
+Baseline run on 2026-11-23:
+
+- Command: `bundle exec rspec spec/models --format progress --profile 20`
+- Result: 1795 examples, 0 failures
+- RSpec time: 3 minutes 0 seconds
+- Load time: 3.59 seconds
+- Random seed: 53882
+
+The initial profile showed repeated setup/teardown as a likely common cost:
+
+- Every example ran route reload, Sidekiq inline setup, a DNS guard, and cache/Redis cleanup.
+- Cache/Redis cleanup was much broader than the model specs that explicitly use cache or Redis state.
+- Slowest example groups included trends/statuses, account statuses cleanup policy, tag feeds, notifications, and status threading concerns.
+- Slowest isolated examples included Trends::Statuses refresh (3.42 seconds), media MP3 metadata (2.89 seconds), and Trends::Statuses queries (about 1.2 seconds each).
+
+Future model runs should preserve the same seed when comparing wall time and RSpec time. Seed variability mostly affects cache/transaction interleaving, not setup costs.
+
+## Optimization rules for model specs
+
+- Do not change factories in this optimization phase.
+- Do not change app runtime code.
+- Use RSpec metadata and shared setup/teardown rather than changing assertions or combining examples.
+- `:uses_cache` marks model examples that need Rails cache isolation.
+- `:uses_redis` marks model examples that need Redis isolation.
+- `:sidekiq_fake` marks model groups that switch Sidekiq to fake mode; suite setup keeps inline mode as the default.
